/*--------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/*-------------------------- File dictionary.dox ---------------------------*/
/*--------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/** \addtogroup area_dictionary */
/** \defgroup theory_dictionary Theory of dictionaries
 \ingroup area_dictionary

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/dictionary` realize the DICTIONARY of
 chapter 7 of the book, i.e., the table with direct access, the hash functions
 of section 7.1 and the hash tables of sections 7.2 to 7.4, and, beyond the
 book, the engineered hash tables, the universal and the perfect hashing, the
 skip list and the digital search trees of strings. This page collects what the
 headers take for granted: the definitions and the notation shared by all of
 them (see \ref theory_dictionary_notation), the realizations and the costs of
 their operations (see \ref theory_dictionary_repr), and, for each family, the
 property on which its correctness rests and the argument behind its bound
 (from \ref theory_dictionary_hash to \ref theory_dictionary_tries). The
 pseudocode of each algorithm is instead on the page of its header, in the
 comment of its function, together with the section of the book, or the paper,
 that it follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_dictionary_notation Definitions and notation

 A DICTIONARY is a univocal relation from a set of keys to a set of values
 (section 3.2.3 of the book), with the operations `lookup()`, `insert()` and
 `remove()`; the keys are drawn from a universe \f$ U \f$, and the dictionary
 holds \f$ n \f$ of them at a time. When \f$ U \f$ is a small interval of
 integers, the table with direct access (`DirectTable.h`) keeps a place for
 each key of \f$ U \f$; otherwise a <em>hash function</em>
 \f$ H : U \rightarrow \{ 0 , \ldots , m - 1 \} \f$ maps the keys to the
 \f$ m \f$ places of a table, and two distinct keys \f$ k \ne k' \f$ with
 \f$ H( k ) = H( k' ) \f$ make a <em>collision</em>, which the table must
 resolve, either by lists of overflow (<em>chaining</em>) or by further
 places of the table itself (<em>open addressing</em>). A function is
 <em>perfect</em> on a set \f$ S \f$ if it has no collision on \f$ S \f$, and
 <em>minimal</em> if moreover \f$ m = | S | \f$.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ U \f$, \f$ S \f$</td>
     <td>the universe of the keys, and the set of the keys in the
         dictionary</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ n \f$, \f$ m \f$</td>
     <td>the number of the keys, and the number of the places (or of the
         lists) of a table</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \alpha = n / m \f$</td>
     <td>the <em>load factor</em> of a table</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ h( k ) \f$, \f$ H( k ) \f$</td>
     <td>the hash of a key, a word of the type <tt>std::size_t</tt> or of 64
         bits, and its place in a table of \f$ m \f$ places, often
         \f$ H( k ) = h( k ) \bmod m \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ H( k , i ) \f$</td>
     <td>the \f$ i \f$-th place of the <em>inspection</em> of \f$ k \f$ in
         a table with open addressing, \f$ 0 \le i < m \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ I( \alpha ) \f$, \f$ S( \alpha ) \f$</td>
     <td>the expected places examined by an unsuccessful and by a
         successful search</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathcal{H} \f$</td>
     <td>a class of hash functions from which one is drawn at
         random</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \sigma \f$, \f$ |k| \f$</td>
     <td>the size of the alphabet of the strings, and the length of the
         key \f$ k \f$</td></tr>
 </table>

 A function \f$ H \f$ has the <em>simple uniformity</em> if each place
 receives the same share of the keys, weighted by the probability
 \f$ P( k ) \f$ that the key \f$ k \f$ is in the dictionary,
 \f[
   \sum_{k : H( k ) = i} P( k ) = \frac{1}{m}
   \quad \forall i \in \{ 0 , \ldots , m - 1 \} , \tag{1}
 \f]
 which no fixed function guarantees for every distribution of the keys; the
 analyses of the tables below assume (1), or, more honestly, a function drawn
 at random from a class (see \ref theory_dictionary_hash).

 \section theory_dictionary_repr Realizations and their costs

 The realizations differ in the time of the operations, in the space and in
 what they offer besides the three operations of the DICTIONARY (the keys in
 order, a search by prefix, a static set).

 <table>
 <caption>The realizations of the DICTIONARY and the cost of their
          operations</caption>
 <tr><th>Realization</th><th><tt>lookup()</tt></th>
     <th><tt>insert()</tt>, <tt>remove()</tt></th><th>Space</th>
     <th>Keys in order</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>DirectTable</tt></td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ \Theta( | U | ) \f$</td><td>yes</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>SortedArrayDictionary</tt></td><td>\f$ O( \log n ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n ) \f$</td><td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td><td>yes</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>SkipList</tt></td><td>\f$ O( \log n ) \f$ expected</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \log n ) \f$ expected</td><td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$
     expected</td><td>yes</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ChainedHashTable</tt></td><td>\f$ O( 1 + \alpha ) \f$
     expected</td><td>\f$ O( 1 + \alpha ) \f$ expected, amortized if it
     restructures itself</td><td>\f$ \Theta( n + m ) \f$</td><td>no</td></tr>
