         <tt>BrentHashTable</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 / ( 1 - \alpha ) ) \f$ expected, \f$ \alpha < 1 \f$</td>
     <td>as <tt>lookup()</tt></td><td>\f$ \Theta( m ) \f$</td>
     <td>no</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>FlatHashTable</tt>, <tt>DenseHashMap</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ expected, \f$ \alpha \le 7 / 8 \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ expected amortized</td><td>\f$ \Theta( m ) \f$</td>
     <td>no</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>CuckooHashTable</tt>, <tt>BucketCuckooTable</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ in the worst case</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ expected amortized</td><td>\f$ \Theta( m ) \f$</td>
     <td>no</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>DLeftHashTable</tt></td><td>\f$ O( d \log \log n ) \f$ with high
     probability</td><td>as <tt>lookup()</tt></td><td>\f$ \Theta( n + m )
     \f$</td><td>no</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>PerfectHashTable</tt> (static)</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ in the worst case</td><td>none, built in
     \f$ O( n ) \f$ expected</td><td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td><td>no</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>Trie</tt>, <tt>RadixTree</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ O( |k| \log \sigma ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( |k| \log \sigma ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( N ) \f$, \f$ O( n ) \f$ nodes for <tt>RadixTree</tt></td>
     <td>yes</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>AdaptiveRadixTree</tt></td><td>\f$ O( |k| ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( |k| ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( n ) \f$ nodes and the keys</td>
     <td>yes</td></tr>
 </table>

 Here \f$ N \f$ is the total length of the keys. The table with direct
 access is preferable when \f$ | U | \f$ is of the order of \f$ n \f$; the
 sorted vector when the lookups far outnumber the changes, or the keys are
 wanted in order; a hash table in every other case, with the engineered tables
 (`FlatHashTable`, `DenseHashMap`, `BucketCuckooTable`) when the speed counts,
 since they compare the fingerprints of a whole group of places at once and
 touch one or two lines of cache per search, against a pointer per cell of the
 chaining and a cluster to walk of the linear inspection. The cuckoo tables are
 preferable when the lookups must be fast in the worst case, the perfect
 hashing when the set is static, and the tries when the keys are strings to
 be searched by prefix or kept in order.

 \section theory_dictionary_hash Hash functions and universal hashing

 The functions of section 7.1 (`HashFunctions.h`) read a key as an integer
 \f$ i \f$ and reduce it to \f$ \{ 0 , \ldots , m - 1 \} \f$: by the
 <em>division</em>, \f$ i \bmod m \f$, best with \f$ m \f$ a prime far from a
 power of 2, whose remainder depends on all the bits of \f$ i \f$; by the
 <em>multiplication</em>, \f$ \lfloor m \, \mathrm{frac}( C i ) \rfloor \f$,
 which works with any \f$ m \f$ and, for \f$ C = ( \sqrt{5} - 1 ) / 2 \f$,
 spreads the consecutive keys most evenly, since the points
 \f$ \mathrm{frac}( C i ) \f$ for \f$ i = 1 , 2 , \ldots \f$ always fall in the
 largest gap left by the previous ones (section 6.4 of TAOCP); and by the
 extraction, the XOR and the middle square of blocks of bits. A fixed function,
 however good, has a set of \f$ n \f$ keys that it sends into one place, when
 \f$ |U| \ge n m \f$ (by the pigeonhole principle); hence the bounds in the
 worst case of the input need a function drawn at random after the keys are
 fixed.

 A class \f$ \mathcal{H} \f$ of functions
 \f$ U \rightarrow \{ 0 , \ldots , m - 1 \} \f$ is <em>universal</em> if
 \f[
   \Pr_{H \in \mathcal{H}}[ H( k ) = H( k' ) ] \le \frac{1}{m}
   \quad \forall k \ne k' \in U , \tag{2}
 \f]
 and <em>\f$ c \f$-universal</em> with \f$ c / m \f$ in place of \f$ 1 / m \f$
 (`UniversalHashing.h`).

 <em>Lemma 1 (Carter and Wegman).</em> With \f$ p \f$ a prime larger than the
 keys, the class of the functions
 \f$ h_{a,b}( k ) = ( ( a k + b ) \bmod p ) \bmod m \f$, \f$ 1 \le a < p \f$
 and \f$ 0 \le b < p \f$, is universal.

 Indeed, for \f$ k \ne k' \f$ the map from \f$ ( a , b ) \f$ to
 \f$ ( r , s ) = ( ( a k + b ) \bmod p , ( a k' + b ) \bmod p ) \f$ is a
 bijection between the \f$ p ( p - 1 ) \f$ pairs with \f$ a \ne 0 \f$ and the
 pairs with \f$ r \ne s \f$, since \f$ \mathbb{Z}_p \f$ is a field and
 \f$ r - s = a ( k - k' ) \f$ determines \f$ a \f$. For each \f$ r \f$, at most
 \f$ \lceil p / m \rceil - 1 \le ( p - 1 ) / m \f$ values \f$ s \ne r \f$ have
 \f$ s \equiv r \pmod{m} \f$; hence at most \f$ p ( p - 1 ) / m \f$ of the
 functions make \f$ k \f$ and \f$ k' \f$ collide, which is (2). The same
 argument on the digits of the keys gives the class of the dot products, and
 the class of Dietzfelbinger et al.,
 \f$ ( a k \bmod 2^h ) \operatorname{div} 2^{h - l} \f$ with \f$ a \f$ odd, is
 2-universal by an argument on the lowest bit in which \f$ k \f$ and
 \f$ k' \f$ differ, with a product and a shift instead of a division.

 By (2), the expected number of the other keys in the list of a key \f$ k \f$
 is \f$ \sum_{k' \ne k} \Pr[ H( k ) = H( k' ) ] \le ( n - 1 ) / m < \alpha \f$,
 whatever the keys; hence every bound of the chaining below holds in
 expectation over the function, for every set of keys. The engineered tables
 use instead the folded product of `FastHash.h`, the two halves of the product
 of two words XORed, and the simple tabulation of `RandomHashing.h`, the XOR of
 a random word per byte of the key, which is only 3-independent but gives the
 linear inspection and the cuckoo hashing the bounds of a fully random function
 (Pătrașcu and Thorup).

 \section theory_dictionary_chaining Hash tables with lists of overflow

 In the table with lists of overflow (section 7.2.1, `ChainedHashTable.h`)
 the place \f$ i \f$ holds the list of the keys with \f$ H( k ) = i \f$.

 <em>Lemma 2 (the cost of the chaining).</em> Under (1), or with a function
 drawn from a universal class, an unsuccessful search examines on average
 \f[
   I( \alpha ) = 1 + \alpha , \qquad
   S( \alpha ) = 1 + \frac{n - 1}{2 m} \approx 1 + \frac{\alpha}{2} \tag{3}
 \f]
 cells, the nil that ends the unsuccessful search counted.

 Indeed, an unsuccessful search scans a whole list, of expected length
 \f$ \alpha \f$, and its end. A successful search of the key inserted
 \f$ j \f$-th scans the keys inserted after it in its list, since each one was
 put at the head, i.e., \f$ ( n - j ) / m \f$ of them on average; the mean of
 \f$ 1 + ( n - j ) / m \f$ over \f$ j = 1 , \ldots , n \f$ is (3). A list that
 moves the key found to its head (exercise 7.5) keeps the keys looked up often
 near the head, and a sorted list (exercise 7.10) stops an unsuccessful search
 at the first larger key, halving its cost on average.

 <em>Lemma 3 (the restructuring).</em> If a table doubles \f$ m \f$ when
 \f$ \alpha \f$ exceeds a threshold \f$ \alpha_{\max} \f$ and halves it when
 \f$ \alpha \f$ falls below \f$ \alpha_{\min} \le \alpha_{\max} / 4 \f$, any
 sequence of operations costs \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized time each, beyond the
 search of each operation (section 7.4).

 Indeed, a restructuring of a table of \f$ m \f$ places costs
 \f$ O( n + m ) = O( m ) \f$, and it leaves the load factor at
 \f$ \alpha_{\max} / 2 \f$ or at \f$ 2 \alpha_{\min} \f$, both between the two
 thresholds by a factor of at least 2; the next restructuring needs the load to
 double or to halve, i.e., \f$ \Omega( m ) \f$ insertions or removals, which
 pay for it. With the thresholds of the example of the book, \f$ 1 / 2 \f$ and
 \f$ 1 / 4 \f$, a table just doubled sits at the lower threshold, and a
 sequence that alternates an insertion and a removal pays \f$ O( m ) \f$ per
 operation; the tables of the library keep the thresholds 4 times apart
 (`TableSize.h`), and grow to a prime size when the inspection needs one.

 \section theory_dictionary_open Open addressing

 With open addressing (section 7.2.2, `OpenHashTable.h`) the keys lie in the
 places of the table, each key \f$ k \f$ in the first free place of its
 inspection \f$ H( k , 0 ) , H( k , 1 ) , \ldots \f$; a removal leaves a
 <em>deleted</em> place, which does not stop the inspections of the other keys
 and is reused by the insertions. Hence \f$ \alpha < 1 \f$, and the cost of a
 search depends on how the inspections of different keys overlap.

 <em>Lemma 4 (uniform hashing).</em> If the inspection of each key is a
 random permutation of the places, independent of the others, then
 \f[
   I( \alpha ) \le \frac{1}{1 - \alpha} , \qquad
   S( \alpha ) \le \frac{1}{\alpha} \ln \frac{1}{1 - \alpha} . \tag{4}
 \f]

 Indeed, an unsuccessful search examines more than \f$ i \f$ places only if
 its first \f$ i \f$ places are full, which has probability at most
 \f$ \alpha^i \f$; hence \f$ I( \alpha ) \le \sum_{i \ge 0} \alpha^i \f$. A
 successful search of a key follows the inspection that its insertion
 followed, which was an unsuccessful search in a table of load
 \f$ j / m \f$ for the \f$ ( j + 1 ) \f$-th key; the mean over
 \f$ j = 0 , \ldots , n - 1 \f$ of \f$ m / ( m - j ) \f$ is at most
 \f$ \frac{1}{\alpha} \int_0^\alpha \frac{dx}{1 - x} \f$, the second bound of
 (4).

 <table>
 <caption>The inspections of <tt>OpenHashTable</tt> and how they
          compare</caption>
 <tr><th>Inspection</th><th>\f$ H( k , i ) \f$</th><th>Sizes \f$ m \f$</th>
     <th>Behavior</th></tr>
 <tr><td>linear</td><td>\f$ ( H_0( k ) + \delta i ) \bmod m \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ \gcd( \delta , m ) = 1 \f$</td>
     <td>primary clustering: \f$ I( \alpha ) \approx \frac{1}{2} ( 1 +
     \frac{1}{( 1 - \alpha )^2} ) \f$, but consecutive places in the
     cache</td></tr>
 <tr><td>quadratic</td><td>\f$ ( H_0( k ) + c_1 i + c_2 i^2 ) \bmod m \f$</td>
     <td>prime</td><td>secondary clustering only; not a permutation, an
     insertion may fail with free places</td></tr>
 <tr><td>triangular</td><td>\f$ ( H_0( k ) + i ( i + 1 ) / 2 ) \bmod m \f$</td>
     <td>a power of 2</td><td>a permutation, secondary clustering
     only</td></tr>
 <tr><td>double hashing</td><td>\f$ ( H_0( k ) + H_\delta( k ) i ) \bmod m
     \f$</td><td>prime or a power of 2</td><td>close to (4)</td></tr>
 <tr><td>pseudo-random</td><td>\f$ ( H_0( k ) + p_i ) \bmod m \f$</td>
     <td>any</td><td>no primary clustering</td></tr>
 </table>

 The linear inspection makes the full places gather in runs, and a key that
 falls anywhere in a run lengthens it; this is the primary clustering, whose
 cost Knuth computed exactly (the formula in the table). The quadratic and the
 triangular inspections spread the keys of different \f$ H_0 \f$, while the
 keys with the same \f$ H_0 \f$ still share their whole inspection (secondary
 clustering). The triangular inspection visits all the places when \f$ m \f$ is
 a power of 2: if \f$ i ( i + 1 ) / 2 \equiv j ( j + 1 ) / 2 \pmod{m} \f$ with
 \f$ 0 \le j < i < m \f$, then \f$ ( i - j ) ( i + j + 1 ) \equiv 0
 \pmod{2 m} \f$, where one factor is odd and the other is positive and below
 \f$ 2 m \f$, a contradiction. The double hashing comes close to uniform
 hashing, since the inspections of two keys coincide only if both
 \f$ H_0 \f$ and \f$ H_\delta \f$ do. The improvement of Brent
 (`BrentHashTable` in `KnuthHashing.h`) moves, at an insertion, a key already
 placed a few steps further along its own inspection when this shortens the
 inspection of the new key, and keeps \f$ S( \alpha ) \f$ below 2.5 even in a
 full table; the coalesced chaining of Algorithm 6.4C (`CoalescedHashTable`)
 links the keys of a place inside the table, with a cellar beyond the places
 addressed by the hash.

 The engineered tables (`FlatHashTable.h`, `DenseHashMap.h`) inspect groups of
 15 or 12 places in the triangular order of the groups, compare the
 fingerprints of 8 bits of a whole group with one instruction of SSE2 or NEON,
 and stop a search at the first group whose byte of overflow lacks the bit of
 the key. A search for an absent key then reads one group in the common case,
 since a group was passed by an insertion only if it was full, which at a load
 of \f$ 7 / 8 \f$ happens to few groups; the removals leave no deleted mark,
 the bits of overflow staying set until the next rebuild, which Lemma 3 pays
 for. `PointerTable` keeps the pointers of the closed list of a search with
 their whole hashes, by the linear inspection.

 \section theory_dictionary_cuckoo Cuckoo hashing

 The cuckoo hashing of Pagh and Rodler (`CuckooHashing.h`) gives each key two
 places, \f$ h_1( k ) \f$ and \f$ h_2( k ) \f$, and keeps it in one of them;
 a lookup and a removal examine two places, in the worst case. An insertion
 puts the key in a free one of its places, or evicts the key in
 \f$ h_1( k ) \f$, which moves to its other place, possibly evicting another
 key, and so on. The <em>cuckoo graph</em> has the places as vertices and an
 edge \f$ \{ h_1( k ) , h_2( k ) \} \f$ per key; a placement is an orientation
 of the edges in which each vertex receives at most one edge.

 <em>Lemma 5 (the cuckoo graph).</em> The keys can be placed if and only if
 each connected component of the cuckoo graph has at most one cycle, and the
 walk of the evictions finds the placement whenever there is one. With
 \f$ m \ge 2 ( 1 + \varepsilon ) n \f$ places and random functions, two places
 are joined by a shortest path of length \f$ L \f$ with probability at most
 \f$ c^{-L} / m \f$, \f$ c = 1 + \varepsilon \f$ (chapter 8 of Ferragina);
 hence an insertion moves \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ keys in expectation, and a rehash is
 needed with probability \f$ O( 1 / n ) \f$.

 Indeed, a component of \f$ v \f$ vertices and \f$ e \f$ edges can host its
 keys only if \f$ e \le v \f$, i.e., if it is a tree or has one cycle; and in
 such a component the evictions, which follow a path and turn around at most
 once at the cycle, end at a free vertex. The expected length of the walk is
 bounded by the expected size of the component of the new key, which the bound
 on the paths makes \f$ O( 1 ) \f$, while the probability that the final graph
 has a component with two cycles sums the same bound over the pairs of places.
 A stash of \f$ s \f$ places (Kirsch, Mitzenmacher and Wieder) keeps the keys
 whose insertion fails, lowering the probability of a rehash to
 \f$ O( 1 / n^{s+1} ) \f$. The buckets of 4 places of `BucketCuckooTable.h`
 accept a load of 95 % and more, with a search in breadth of the shortest
 path of evictions instead of the random walk; the d-left hashing of
 `DLeftHashTable` puts a key in the shortest of its \f$ d \f$ lists, which
 makes the longest list \f$ \ln \ln n / ( d \ln \phi_d ) + O( 1 ) \f$ with high
 probability instead of \f$ \Theta( \log n / \log \log n ) \f$, the power of
 two choices (Azar, Broder, Karlin and Upfal; Vöcking).

 \section theory_dictionary_perfect Perfect hashing

 On a static set \f$ S \f$ a perfect function gives lookups in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$
 time in the worst case (`PerfectHashing.h`).

 <em>Lemma 6 (Fredman, Komlós and Szemerédi).</em> With \f$ h \f$ drawn from a
 universal class into \f$ n \f$ lists, \f$ n_j \f$ the keys of the list
 \f$ j \f$,
 \f[
   E\Bigl[ \sum_j n_j^2 \Bigr] < 2 n , \tag{5}
 \f]
 and a function drawn from a universal class into \f$ n_j^2 \f$ places has no
 collision on the \f$ n_j \f$ keys with probability more than \f$ 1 / 2 \f$.

 Indeed, \f$ \sum_j n_j^2 = n + 2 C \f$, \f$ C \f$ the number of the pairs of
 keys that collide, and by (2)
 \f$ E[ C ] \le \binom{n}{2} / n = ( n - 1 ) / 2 \f$, which gives (5). In the
 same way, \f$ n_j \f$ keys in \f$ n_j^2 \f$ places collide in
 \f$ \binom{n_j}{2} / n_j^2 < 1 / 2 \f$ pairs on average, and by the inequality
 of Markov there is no collision with probability more than \f$ 1 / 2 \f$;
 hence each table of the second level is found after fewer than 2 trials on
 average, and the two levels take \f$ O( n ) \f$ places. The first level
 of `PerfectHashTable` is drawn until \f$ \sum_j n_j^2 < 4 n \f$: since
 \f$ \sum_j n_j^2 - n = 2 C \ge 0 \f$ has expectation below \f$ n \f$, the
 inequality of Markov gives \f$ \sum_j n_j^2 \ge 4 n \f$ with probability
 below \f$ 1 / 3 \f$, so that \f$ h \f$ is drawn fewer than \f$ 3 / 2 \f$
 times on average and the second level takes fewer than \f$ 4 n \f$ places
 (a threshold of \f$ 2 n \f$, the mean itself, would be met with a
 probability that the inequality of Markov does not bound away from 0).

 A minimal perfect function needs at least \f$ n \log_2 e \approx 1.44 n \f$
 bits: a random function from \f$ U \f$ to \f$ \{ 0 , \ldots , n - 1 \} \f$ is
 minimal perfect on \f$ S \f$ with probability \f$ n! / n^n \approx e^{-n}
 \sqrt{2 \pi n} \f$, so that a family that serves every \f$ S \f$ must have
 about \f$ e^n \f$ members. The schemes of the area approach the bound at
 different costs.

 <table>
 <caption>The minimal perfect hash functions of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Scheme</th><th>Header</th><th>Bits per key</th>
     <th>Build</th><th>Evaluation</th></tr>
 <tr><td>Czech, Havas and Majewski, order preserving</td>
     <td><tt>PerfectHashing.h</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ c \lceil \log_2 n \rceil \f$, \f$ c > 2 \f$ or
         \f$ c > 1.23 \f$</td><td>\f$ O( n ) \f$ expected</td>
     <td>2 or 3 accesses</td></tr>
 <tr><td>fingerprinting (BBHash, FMPH)</td>
     <td><tt>FingerprintPerfectHash.h</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ \gamma e^{1 / \gamma} \f$, 3.3 for \f$ \gamma = 2 \f$</td>
     <td>a few passes</td><td>\f$ e^{1 / \gamma} \f$ levels expected</td></tr>
 <tr><td>pilots (PTHash)</td><td><tt>MinimalPerfectHash.h</tt></td>
     <td>about 3</td><td>slower</td><td>2 accesses</td></tr>
 <tr><td>recursive splitting (RecSplit)</td><td><tt>RecSplit.h</tt></td>
     <td>about 1.8</td><td>slowest, \f$ e^\ell \f$ per leaf</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \log ( b / \ell ) ) \f$ levels</td></tr>
 </table>

 The scheme of Czech, Havas and Majewski maps each key to an edge of a random
 graph of \f$ c n \f$ vertices and asks for the values \f$ g \f$ of the
 vertices such that \f$ g( u ) + g( v ) \equiv i \pmod{n} \f$ on the edge of
 the \f$ i \f$-th key: on a forest, a visit from a root with \f$ g = 0 \f$
 assigns each vertex once, and the equations hold; a random graph is acyclic
 with probability about \f$ \sqrt{( c - 2 ) / c} \f$ for \f$ c > 2 \f$. With
 three vertices per key the equations are solved in the reverse order of the
 <em>peeling</em>, the repeated removal of a vertex of degree 1 with its edge,
 which succeeds if the hypergraph has no 2-core, i.e., for \f$ c > 1.23 \f$:
 each vertex removed is assigned after the other vertices of its edge, and no
 edge assigned later contains it. The fingerprinting keeps, at each level, a
 bit per place set when exactly one key falls there, and sends the keys that
 collide to the next level; a key is alone in its place with probability
 \f$ e^{-1 / \gamma} \f$, which gives the geometric series of its header.
 PTHash places the buckets of keys from the largest, trying for each one the
 integers \f$ k_b = 0 , 1 , \ldots \f$ as pilots until its keys fall in free
 places; RecSplit splits each bucket recursively until its leaves of
 \f$ \ell \f$ keys can be mapped bijectively by a seed, and writes the seeds
 by a code of Golomb and Rice, whose total length approaches the bound since
 the product of the probabilities of the seeds of a tree is \f$ m! / m^m \f$
 whatever its shape.

 \section theory_dictionary_tries Ordered dictionaries and tries

 The sorted vector (`SortedArrayDictionary.h`) finds a key by the binary
 search of section 1.2 in \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ and moves the pairs after it at
 a change. The skip list of Pugh (`SkipList.h`) keeps the keys in a sorted list
 with express lanes, each node in the levels \f$ 0 , \ldots , \ell - 1 \f$ with
 \f$ \Pr[ \ell \ge j + 1 ] = 2^{-j} \f$.

 <em>Lemma 7 (the skip list).</em> A search in a skip list of \f$ n \f$ keys
 examines \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ nodes in expectation, and the list takes
 \f$ 2 n \f$ links in expectation.

 Indeed, the level \f$ j \f$ holds \f$ n / 2^j \f$ nodes in expectation, so
 that the levels above \f$ \log_2 n \f$ hold \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ nodes in all and
 the links are \f$ \sum_j n / 2^j = 2 n \f$. The path of a search, read
 backwards from the node where it ends, goes up a level at each node with
 probability \f$ 1 / 2 \f$ (the node has a further level) and left otherwise;
 hence it makes 2 steps per level in expectation, and \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ in
 all.

 A <em>trie</em> (`Trie.h`) stands for a set of strings by a tree whose node
 at depth \f$ d \f$ is a prefix of \f$ d \f$ characters, the children indexed
 by the next character and the nodes that end a key marked: a search reads one
 node per character, \f$ O( |k| \log \sigma ) \f$ with the children in a
 sorted map, whatever \f$ n \f$, and the keys with a given prefix are the
 marked nodes below the node of the prefix. The compact trie (`RadixTree`)
 merges each chain of unmarked nodes with one child into an edge labeled by a
 string; every unmarked node then has at least two children, so that it has at
 most \f$ n - 1 \f$ unmarked nodes, and \f$ 2 n \f$ nodes in all. The Patricia
 trie of `PatriciaTrie.h` drops the labels and keeps in each node only the
 length of its prefix and the first character of each edge: its <em>blind
 search</em> goes down by the branching characters alone, reads the one string
 of a leaf below the point reached, and finds from the longest common prefix
 \f$ \ell \f$ of that string and of the pattern the edge where the pattern
 leaves the trie. This is correct since the leaf reached shares with the
 pattern a prefix as long as any other string does: the first \f$ \ell \f$
 characters of the pattern drive the descent along the path of that prefix, up
 to the node of depth at most \f$ \ell \f$ where they part. It reads one string
 instead of a label per edge, \f$ O( p / B ) \f$ transfers of blocks for a
 pattern of \f$ p \f$ characters (`TwoLevelDictionary.h` puts it over the front
 coding of the strings). The adaptive radix tree of Leis, Kemper and Neumann
 (`AdaptiveRadixTree.h`) branches on the bytes, with the layout of each node
 adapted to the number of its children (4, 16, 48 or 256), the path
 compression of the chains and the whole key compared at the leaf, so that a
 search takes \f$ O( |k| ) \f$ with \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ per node.

 The Patricia trie in succinct space (`SuccinctPatriciaTrie.h`) codes the shape
 of the compact trie of a static dictionary by the degrees of its nodes in
 depth (DFUDS) of Benoit, Demaine, Munro, Raman, Raman and Rao: the nodes are
 visited in depth, the children by their first characters, and a node of \f$ d
 \f$ children is written as \f$ d \f$ open parentheses and a closed one, after
 an open parenthesis that balances the whole, i.e., \f$ 2 N \f$ bits for \f$ N
 \le 2 n \f$ nodes; the first character of each edge goes in a byte, in the
 order of the open parentheses, and the rest of its label in a string apart.
 The node of rank \f$ v \f$ in the visit begins after the \f$ v \f$-th closed
 parenthesis, and its \f$ i \f$-th child, for the node at the place \f$ p \f$
 with \f$ d \f$ children, begins after the closed parenthesis that matches the
 open one at \f$ p + d - 1 - i \f$ (the last open parenthesis of the node is
 matched by the closed one that ends it, right after which the first child
 begins); the characters of the children of a node are consecutive, and the
 child of a character is found by a binary search on them.

 <em>Lemma 8 (the subtree in DFUDS).</em> The parentheses of the subtree of
 the node at the place \f$ p \f$ are those from \f$ p \f$ to the first place
 \f$ e \f$ whose excess is one less than the one at \f$ p - 1 \f$, and the
 nodes of the subtree are the ranks from \f$ v \f$ to the number of closed
 parentheses up to \f$ e \f$.

 Indeed, an open parenthesis followed by the code of a subtree is the code of
 that subtree as a whole tree, which is balanced: its excess comes back to the
 level before the open parenthesis only at its end. The code of the subtree
 alone thus goes one level below the excess at \f$ p - 1 \f$ for the first
 time at its last parenthesis, and the visit in depth writes the nodes of the
 subtree one after the other, from \f$ v \f$ on. Hence, with a vector of bits
 that marks the nodes where a string ends, the rank of a string among the
 marks is its rank in the lexicographic order (a node comes before its
 subtree, which holds the strings it prefixes, and the subtrees follow the
 order of their first characters), and the strings with a prefix \f$ P \f$
 are the marks of the subtree where the walk of \f$ P \f$ ends, a range of
 ranks found by one forward search; a lookup walks down \f$ O( | Q | ) \f$
 nodes and the string of a rank walks up by matching the closed parenthesis
 before each node. The space is \f$ 2 N + o( N ) \f$ bits for the shape,
 \f$ 8 ( N - 1 ) \f$ for the first characters, \f$ 2 N + o( N ) \f$ for the
 marks of the ends and of the nodes with a rest, and the rests themselves,
 with their places by Elias-Fano. The path-decomposed tries of Grossi and
 Ottaviano and the dictionaries of Martínez-Prieto et al. compress the labels
 as well (by Re-Pair or by Hu-Tucker codes), which is not done here.

